The key observation is still:

At any point, one half of the array is always sorted.

Here's the explanation in the same simple style.

Step 1: Check which half is sorted

If nums[mid] >= nums[left], then mid belongs to the left sorted part.

That means all the elements from left to mid are in increasing order.

Now ask yourself:

Can the target be inside this sorted range?

If

nums[left] <= target && target < nums[mid]

then the target lies inside the left sorted part.

So we search the left half.

right = mid - 1;

Otherwise, the target cannot be in the left sorted part.

So we search the right half.

left = mid + 1;
Step 2: Otherwise, the right half is sorted

If nums[mid] < nums[left], then mid belongs to the right sorted part.

That means all the elements from mid to right are in increasing order.

Now ask yourself:

Can the target be inside this sorted range?

If

nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[right]

then the target lies inside the right sorted part.

So we search the right half.

left = mid + 1;

Otherwise, the target cannot be in the right sorted part.

So we search the left half.

right = mid - 1;
Step 3: Continue until the target is found

At every iteration:

Find mid.
Check whether the left half or right half is sorted.
Check whether the target lies inside that sorted half.
If it does, search that half.
Otherwise, search the other half.

Each time, we discard half of the remaining search space.

Eventually:

If nums[mid] == target, we return its index.
If left > right, the target is not present, so we return -1.
Why does this work?

In a rotated sorted array, both halves cannot be unsorted at the same time.

At least one half is always sorted.

That sorted half tells us the exact range of values it contains.

If the target falls inside that range, we search there.

Otherwise, we know for certain the target must be in the other half.

By making this decision every iteration, we discard half of the array each time, just like normal binary search.

Think of it like this

For Find Minimum:

Which half contains the minimum element?

For Search Target:

Which half is sorted, and does that sorted half contain my target?

That's the only extra question you ask. Everything else is still binary search.
